  "Number": 33,
  "Question": "If \\(9^{x+2}=240+9^x,\\) then the value of \\(x\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0.1\\)",
    "B": "\\(0.2\\)",
    "C": "\\(0.3\\)",
    "D": "\\(0.4\\)",
    "E": "\\(0.5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Both sides contain a power of \\(9\\), so rewrite the larger power to expose the common quantity. Because the base is positive, the exponent law gives \\(9^{x+2}=9^x\\cdot9^2=81\\cdot9^x\\). Hence\n\\[\n81\\cdot9^x=240+9^x,\n\\qquad\n80\\cdot9^x=240,\n\\qquad\n9^x=3.\n\\]\nNow \\(3=\\sqrt9=9^{1/2}\\). Since \\(9>1\\), the function \\(9^x\\) is strictly increasing, so equal powers of this base have equal exponents. Therefore \\(x=\\tfrac12=\\boxed{0.5}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
